#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7743
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:10463
#, no-wrap
msgid "sp"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7744
#, no-wrap
msgid "Scaled points"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7746
msgid "scaled point (65536sp = 1pt)"
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7748
#, no-wrap
msgid "ridt.eps"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7751
msgid ""
"For example, the following will scale a file @file{ridt.eps} to one inch "
"vertically, with the width scaled proportionately:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7754
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@image@{ridt,,1in@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7756
#, no-wrap
msgid "epsf.tex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7763
msgid ""
"For @code{@@image} to work with @TeX{}, the file @file{epsf.tex} must be "
"installed somewhere that @TeX{} can find it.  (The standard location is "
"@file{@var{texmf}/tex/generic/dvips/epsf.tex}, where @var{texmf} is a root "
"of your @TeX{} directory tree.)  This file is included in the Texinfo "
"distribution and is also available from @uref{ftp://tug.org/tex/epsf.tex}, "
"among other places."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7768
msgid ""
"@code{@@image} can be used within a line as well as for displayed figures.  "
"Therefore, if you intend it to be displayed, be sure to leave a blank line "
"before the command, or the output will run into the preceding text."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7771
msgid ""
"Image scaling is presently implemented only in printed output, not in any "
"other sort of output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7773
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7775
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7776
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnotes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7775
msgid "@nodedescription Writing footnotes."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7777
#, no-wrap
msgid "footnote"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7785
msgid ""
"A @dfn{footnote} is for a reference that documents or elucidates the primary "
"text.@footnote{A footnote should complement or expand upon the primary text, "
"but a reader should not need to read a footnote to understand the primary "
"text.  For a thorough discussion of footnotes, see @cite{The Chicago Manual "
"of Style}, which is published by the University of Chicago Press.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7790
msgid ""
"Footnotes are distracting; use them sparingly at most, and it is best to "
"avoid them completely.  Standard bibliographical references are usually "
"better placed in a bibliography at the end of a document instead of in "
"footnotes throughout."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7792
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7794
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnote Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7794
msgid "@nodedescription How to write a footnote in Texinfo."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7801
msgid ""
"In Texinfo, footnotes are created with the @code{@@footnote} command.  This "
"command is followed immediately by a left brace, then by the text of the "
"footnote, and then by a terminating right brace.  Footnotes may be of any "
"length (they will be broken across pages if necessary), but are usually "
"short.  The template is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7804
#, no-wrap
msgid "ordinary text@@footnote@{@var{text of footnote}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7809
msgid ""
"As shown here, the @code{@@footnote} command should come right after the "
"text being footnoted, with no intervening space; otherwise, the footnote "
"marker might end up starting a line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7813
msgid ""
"For example, this clause is followed by a sample footnote@footnote{Here is "
"the sample footnote.}; in the Texinfo source, it looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7817
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@dots{}a sample footnote@@footnote@{Here is the sample\n"
"footnote.@}; in the Texinfo source@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7825
msgid ""
"As you can see, this source includes two punctuation marks next to each "
"other; in this case, @samp{.@};} is the sequence.  This is normal (the first "
"ends the footnote and the second belongs to the sentence being footnoted), "
"so don't worry that it looks odd.  (Another style, perfectly acceptable, is "
"to put the footnote after punctuation belonging to the sentence, as in "
"@samp{;@@footnote@{...}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7829
msgid ""
"In printed output formats, the reference mark for a footnote is a small, "
"superscripted number; the text of the footnote appears at the bottom of the "
"page, below a horizontal line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7834
msgid ""
"In Info, the reference mark for a footnote is a pair of parentheses with the "
"footnote number between them, like this: @samp{(1)}.  The reference mark is "
"followed by a cross-reference link to the footnote text if footnotes are put "
"in separate nodes (@pxref{Footnote Styles})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7838
msgid ""
"In the HTML output, footnote references are generally marked with a small, "
"superscripted number which is rendered as a hypertext link to the footnote "
"text."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7839
#, no-wrap
msgid "Critical editions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7840
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nested footnotes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7843
msgid ""
"Footnotes cannot be nested, and cannot appear in section headings of any "
"kind or other ``unusual'' places."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7847
msgid ""
"A final tip: footnotes in the argument of an @code{@@item} command for an "
"@code{@@table} must be entirely on the same line as the @code{@@item} (as "
"usual).  @xref{Two-column Tables}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7849
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7851
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnote Styles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7851
msgid "@nodedescription Controlling how footnotes appear."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7856
msgid ""
"Online formats have two footnote styles, which determine where the text of "
"the footnote is located, the @samp{end} and @samp{separate} footnote style."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7858
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{end} node footnote style"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7859
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnote style @subentry @samp{end}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7860
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18437
#, no-wrap
msgid "end"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7866
msgid ""
"For Info, in the @samp{end} node style, all the footnotes for a single node "
"are placed at the end of that node.  The footnotes are separated from the "
"rest of the node by a line of dashes with the word @samp{Footnotes} within "
"it.  Each footnote begins with an @samp{(@var{n})} reference mark."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7871
msgid ""
"Here is an example of the Info output for a single footnote in the "
"end-of-node style:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7875
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"--------- Footnotes ---------\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7877
#, no-wrap
msgid "(1)  Here is a sample footnote.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7882
msgid ""
"In HTML, when the footnote style is @samp{end}, or if the output is not "
"split, footnotes are put at the end of each output file."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7883
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{separate} footnote style"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7884
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnote style @subentry @samp{separate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7885
#, no-wrap
msgid "separate"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7892
msgid ""
"For Info, in the @samp{separate} node style, all the footnotes for a single "
"node are placed in an automatically constructed node of their own.  In this "
"style, a ``footnote reference'' follows each @samp{(@var{n})} reference mark "
"in the body of the node.  The footnote reference is actually a "
"cross-reference which you use to reach the footnote node."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7899
msgid ""
"The name of the node with the footnotes is constructed by appending "
"@w{@samp{-Footnotes}} to the name of the node that contains the footnotes. "
"(Consequently, the footnotes' node for the @file{Footnotes} node is "
"@w{@file{Footnotes-Footnotes}}!)  The footnotes' node has an `Up' node "
"pointer that leads back to its parent node."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7903
msgid ""
"Here is how the first footnote in this manual looks after being formatted "
"for Info in the separate node style:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7907
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"File: texinfo.info  Node: Overview-Footnotes, Up: Overview\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7910
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(1) The first syllable of \"Texinfo\" is pronounced like\n"
"\"speck\", not \"hex\". @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7915
msgid ""
"In HTML, when the footnote style is @samp{separate}, and the output is "
"split, footnotes are placed in a separate file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7920
msgid ""
"Unless your document has long and important footnotes (as in, say, Gibbon's "
"@cite{Decline and Fall @dots{}}), we recommend the @samp{end} style, as it "
"is simpler for readers to follow."
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7921
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:14776
#, no-wrap
msgid "footnotestyle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7926
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@footnotestyle} command to specify the footnote style.  Write "
"this command at the beginning of a line followed by an argument, either "
"@samp{end} for the end node style or @samp{separate} for the separate node "
"style."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7932
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@footnotestyle end\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: exdent
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7935
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:21068
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:21134
#, no-wrap
msgid "or"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7937
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@footnotestyle separate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7940
msgid "Write a @code{@@footnotestyle} command in the Texinfo file preamble."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7942
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7944
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7945
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7944
msgid "@nodedescription Creating indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7953
msgid ""
"Using Texinfo, you can generate indices without having to sort and collate "
"entries manually.  In an index, the entries are listed in alphabetical "
"order, together with information on how to find the discussion of each "
"entry.  In a printed manual, this information consists of page numbers.  In "
"other formats, links to the index entries location or to the associated "
"nodes are generated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7958
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides several predefined kinds of indices: an index for "
"functions, an index for variables, an index for concepts, and so on.  You "
"can combine indices or use them for other than their canonical purpose.  "
"Lastly, you can define your own new indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7960
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7962
#, no-wrap
msgid "Predefined Indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7962
msgid "@nodedescription Use different indices for different kinds of entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7966
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides six predefined indices.  Here are their nominal meanings, "
"abbreviations, and the corresponding index entry commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7968
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8389
#, no-wrap
msgid "cp"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7969
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{cp} (concept) index"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7970
#, no-wrap
msgid "cindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7971
#, no-wrap
msgid "cpindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7973
msgid "(@code{@@cindex}) Concept index, for general concepts."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7973
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8391
#, no-wrap
msgid "fn"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7974
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{fn} (function) index"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7975
#, no-wrap
msgid "findex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7976
#, no-wrap
msgid "fnindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7979
msgid ""
"(@code{@@findex}) Function index, for function and function-like names (such "
"as entry points of libraries)."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7979
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8393
#, no-wrap
msgid "ky"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7980
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{ky} (keystroke) index"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7981
#, no-wrap
msgid "kindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7982
#, no-wrap
msgid "kyindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7984
msgid "(@code{@@kindex}) Keystroke index, for keyboard commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7984
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8395
#, no-wrap
msgid "pg"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7985
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{pg} (program) index"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7986
#, no-wrap
msgid "pindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7987
#, no-wrap
msgid "pgindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7989
msgid "(@code{@@pindex}) Program index, for names of programs."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7989
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8397
#, no-wrap
msgid "tp"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7990
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{tp} (data type) index"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7991
#, no-wrap
msgid "tindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7992
#, no-wrap
msgid "tpindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7995
msgid ""
"(@code{@@tindex}) Data type index, for type names (such as structures "
"defined in header files)."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7995
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8399
#, no-wrap
msgid "vr"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7996
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{vr} (variable) index"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7997
#, no-wrap
msgid "vindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:7998
#, no-wrap
msgid "vrindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8001
msgid ""
"(@code{@@vindex}) Variable index, for variable names (such as library global "
"variables)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8008
msgid ""
"Not every manual needs all of these, and most manuals use only two or three "
"at most.  The present manual, for example, has two indices: a concept index "
"and an @@-command index. (The latter is actually the function index but is "
"called a command index in the chapter heading.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8016
msgid ""
"You are not required to use the predefined indices strictly for their "
"canonical purposes.  For example, suppose you wish to index some C "
"preprocessor macros.  You could put them in the function index along with "
"actual functions, just by writing @code{@@findex} commands for them; then, "
"when you print the ``Function Index'' as an unnumbered chapter, you could "
"give it the title `Function and Macro Index' and all will be consistent for "
"the reader."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8022
msgid ""
"On the other hand, it is best not to stray too far from the meaning of the "
"predefined indices.  Otherwise, in the event that your text is combined with "
"other text from other manuals, the index entries will not match up.  "
"Instead, define your own new index (@pxref{New Indices})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8028
msgid ""
"We recommend having a single index in the final document whenever possible, "
"however many source indices you use, since then readers have only one place "
"to look.  Two or more source indices can be combined into one output index "
"by using the @code{@@synindex} or @code{@@syncodeindex} commands "
"(@pxref{Combining Indices})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8031
msgid ""
"Index entry commands based on the the two letter index names are also valid, "
"for example @code{@@cpindex} can be used instead of @code{@@cindex}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8033
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indexing Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8035
msgid "@nodedescription How to make an index entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8035
#, no-wrap
msgid "Defining the Entries of an Index"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8037
#, no-wrap
msgid "Defining indexing entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8038
#, no-wrap
msgid "Index entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8039
#, no-wrap
msgid "Entries for an index"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8040
#, no-wrap
msgid "Specifying index entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8041
#, no-wrap
msgid "Creating index entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8047
msgid ""
"The data to make an index come from many individual indexing commands "
"scattered throughout the Texinfo source file.  Each command says to add one "
"entry to a particular index; after formatting, the index will give the "
"current page number or node name as the reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8050
msgid ""
"An index entry consists of an indexing command at the beginning of a line "
"followed, on the rest of the line, by the entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8053
msgid ""
"For example, this section begins with the following five entries for the "
"concept index:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8060
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@cindex Defining indexing entries\n"
"@@cindex Index entries, defining\n"
"@@cindex Entries for an index\n"
"@@cindex Specifying index entries\n"
"@@cindex Creating index entries\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8065
msgid ""
"Each predefined index has its own indexing command---@code{@@cindex} for the "
"concept index, @code{@@findex} for the function index, and so on, as listed "
"in the previous section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8068
msgid ""
"Index entries should precede the visible material that is being indexed.  "
"For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8072
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@cindex hello\n"
"Hello, there!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8077
msgid ""
"whatever context) ends up before the material, where readers want to be, "
"instead of after."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8083
msgid ""
"In Info, an index is usually formatted as a menu.  Try to avoid using a "
"colon in index entries, as this may confuse some Info readers.  @xref{Menu "
"Parts} for more information about the structure of a menu entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8084
#, no-wrap
msgid "Index font types"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8091
msgid ""
"By default, entries for a concept index are printed in a roman font and "
"entries for the other indices are printed in a typewriter font.  You may "
"change the way part of an entry is printed with the usual Texinfo commands, "
"such as @code{@@file} for file names (@pxref{Marking Text}), and @code{@@r} "
"for the normal roman font (@pxref{Fonts})."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8092
#, no-wrap
msgid "sortas"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8093
#, no-wrap
msgid "sort keys for index entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8094
#, no-wrap
msgid "index sorting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8100
msgid ""
"You may specify an explicit sort key for an index entry using "
"@code{@@sortas} following either the index command or the text of the "
"entry.  For example: @samp{@@findex @@sortas@{\\@} \\ @@r@{(literal \\ in "
"@@code@{@@@@math@})} sorts the index entry this produces under backslash."
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8101
#, no-wrap
msgid "txiindexbackslashignore"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8102
#, no-wrap
msgid "txiindexhyphenignore"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8103
#, no-wrap
msgid "txiindexlessthanignore"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8104
#, no-wrap
msgid "txiindexatsignignore"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8117
msgid ""
"You may choose to ignore certain characters in index entries for the "
"purposes of sorting.  The characters that you can currently choose to ignore "
"are @samp{\\}, @samp{-}, @samp{<} and @samp{@@}, which are ignored by giving "
"as an argument to the @code{@@set} command, respectively, "
"@code{txiindexbackslashignore}, @code{txiindexhyphenignore}, "
"@code{txiindexlessthanignore} and @code{txiindexatsignignore}.  For example, "
"specifying @samp{@@set txiindexbackslashignore} causes the "
"@samp{\\mathopsup} entry in the index for this manual to be sorted as if it "
"were @samp{mathopsup}, so that it appears among the other entries beginning "
"with `M'.  This avoids the need to provide explicit sort keys for index "
"entries containing these characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8121
msgid ""
"When using these options, it is possible to get an index entry with an empty "
"sort key.  To avoid this, specify a @code{@@sortas} directive in the index "
"entry.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8125
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@set txiindexbackslashignore\n"
"@@findex @@sortas@{\\@} \\\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8128
#, no-wrap
msgid "Advanced Indexing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8130
msgid "@nodedescription Advanced indexing commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8130
#, no-wrap
msgid "Advanced Indexing Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8131
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indexing, advanced"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8132
#, no-wrap
msgid "Advanced indexing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8135
msgid "Texinfo provides several further commands for indexing."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8136
#, no-wrap
msgid "subentry"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8141
msgid ""
"First, you can create @dfn{multilevel} index entries, allowing you to group "
"many related subtopics under the same higher-level topic.  You do this by "
"separating the parts of such an entry with the @code{@@subentry} command. "
"Such commands might look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8145
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@cindex Superhumans @@subentry villains\n"
"@@cindex Superhumans @@subentry heroes\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8148
msgid "You may have up to three levels in an entry:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8152
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@cindex coffee makers @@subentry electric @@subentry pink\n"
"@@cindex coffee makers @@subentry electric @@subentry blue\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8157
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{@@sortas} command mentioned earlier with any or all of "
"the three parts of an entry to cause them to sort differently than they "
"would by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8158
#, no-wrap
msgid "seeentry"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8161
msgid ""
"Second, you may provide an index entry that points to another, using the "
"@code{@@seeentry} (``see entry'') command. For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8164
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@cindex Indexes @@seeentry@{Indices@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8169
msgid ""
"Such an entry should be unique in your document; the idea is to redirect the "
"reader to the other entry where they will find all the information they are "
"looking for."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8170
#, no-wrap
msgid "seealso"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8174
msgid ""
"Finally, you may provide a ``see also'' entry using the @code{@@seealso} "
"command. These entries go along with regular entries, and are grouped "
"together with them in the final printed index. For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8181
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@cindex Coffee\n"
"@@cindex Coffee @@subentry With milk and sugar\n"
"@@cindex Coffee @@subentry With doughnuts\n"
"@@cindex Coffee @@subentry Decaffeinated\n"
"@@cindex Coffee @@seealso@{Tea@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8188
msgid ""
"When using all three of these advanced commands, @emph{do not} place a comma "
"between the different parts of the index text. The @command{texindex} "
"program, which sorts the index entries and generates the indexing formatting "
"commands, takes care of placing commas in the correct places for you."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8192
msgid ""
"Do not interrupt an index or @code{@@subentry} entry by @code{@@sortas} or "
"advanced commands with braces; place the commands with braces at the "
"beginning or at the end of the entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8195
msgid ""
"These features are the most useful with printed documents, and when "
"translating Texinfo to DocBook."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8197
#, no-wrap
msgid "Index Entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8199
msgid "@nodedescription Choose different words for index entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8199
#, no-wrap
msgid "Making Index Entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8200
#, no-wrap
msgid "Index entries, making"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8201
#, no-wrap
msgid "Entries, making index"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8203
#, no-wrap
msgid "Writing index entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8204
#, no-wrap
msgid "Index entries, advice on writing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8205
#, no-wrap
msgid "Advice on writing entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8206
#, no-wrap
msgid "Capitalization of index entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8213
msgid ""
"Concept index entries consist of text.  The best way to write an index is to "
"devise entries which are terse yet clear.  If you can do this, the index "
"usually looks better if the entries are written just as they would appear in "
"the middle of a sentence, that is, capitalizing only proper names and "
"acronyms that always call for uppercase letters.  This is the case "
"convention we use in most GNU manuals' indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8219
msgid ""
"If you don't see how to make an entry terse yet clear, make it longer and "
"clear---not terse and confusing.  If many of the entries are several words "
"long, the index may look better if you use a different convention: "
"capitalize the first word of each entry.  Whichever case convention you use, "
"use it consistently."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8226
msgid ""
"In any event, do not ever capitalize a case-sensitive name such as a C or "
"Lisp function name or a shell command; that would be a spelling error.  "
"Entries in indices other than the concept index are symbol names in "
"programming languages, or program names; these names are usually "
"case-sensitive, so likewise use upper- and lowercase as required."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8227
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unique index entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8236
msgid ""
"It is a good idea to make index entries unique wherever feasible.  That way, "
"people using the printed output or online completion of index entries don't "
"see undifferentiated lists.  Consider this an opportunity to make "
"otherwise-identical index entries be more specific, so readers can more "
"easily find the exact place they are looking for.  The advanced indexing "
"features described in @ref{Advanced Indexing} can help with this, as well."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8249
msgid ""
"When you are making index entries, it is good practice to think of the "
"different ways people may look for something.  Different people @emph{do "
"not} think of the same words when they look something up.  A helpful index "
"will have items indexed under all the different words that people may use.  "
"For example, one reader may think it obvious that the two-letter names for "
"indices should be listed under ``Indices, two-letter names'', since "
"``Indices'' are the general concept.  But another reader may remember the "
"specific concept of two-letter names and search for the entry listed as "
"``Two letter names for indices''.  A good index will have both entries and "
"will help both readers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8253
msgid ""
"Like typesetting, the construction of an index is a skilled art, the "
"subtleties of which may not be appreciated until you need to do it yourself."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8255
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing Indices & Menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8257
msgid ""
"@nodedescription How to print an index in hardcopy and generate index menus "
"in Info."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8257
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing Indices and Menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8258
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing an index"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8259
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indices, printing and menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8260
#, no-wrap
msgid "Generating menus with indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8261
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menus generated with indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8264
#, no-wrap
msgid "printindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8271
msgid ""
"@code{@@printindex} takes one argument, a two-letter index abbreviation.  "
"You must include the @code{@@printindex} command at the place in the "
"document where you want the index to appear.  This does not happen "
"automatically just because you use @code{@@cindex} or other index-entry "
"generating commands in the Texinfo file; those just cause the raw data for "
"the index to be accumulated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8277
msgid ""
"You should precede the @code{@@printindex} command with a suitable section "
"or chapter command (usually @code{@@appendix} or @code{@@unnumbered}) to "
"supply the chapter heading and put the index into the table of contents.  "
"Precede the chapter heading with an @code{@@node} line as usual."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8284
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Variable Index\n"
"@@unnumbered Variable Index\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8286
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@printindex vr\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8291
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:13872
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Concept Index\n"
"@@unnumbered Concept Index\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8293
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:13874
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@printindex cp\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8298
msgid ""
"The text @samp{Index} needs to appear in the name of the node containing the "
"index for the index to be found by Info readers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8300
msgid ""
"If you have more than one index, we recommend placing the concept index last."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8302
msgid "Other details of index output in output formats:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8309
msgid ""
"As part of the process of creating a printed manual with @TeX{}, you run a "
"program called @command{texindex} (@pxref{Hardcopy with @TeX{}})  to sort "
"the raw data to produce a sorted index file.  The sorted index file is what "
"is actually used to print the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8312
msgid ""
"@code{@@printindex} reads the corresponding sorted index file and produces a "
"traditional two-column index, with index terms and page numbers."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8319
msgid ""
"In Info output, @code{@@printindex} produces a special menu containing the "
"line number of the entry, relative to the start of the node.  Info readers "
"can use this to go to the exact line of an entry, not just the containing "
"node.  (Older Info readers will just go to the node.)  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8322
#, no-wrap
msgid "* First index entry:   Top.   (line  7)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8328
msgid ""
"In plain text output, @code{@@printindex} formatting is usually similar to a "
"menu in Info, showing the line number of each entry relative to the start of "
"the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8332
msgid ""
"In HTML output, @code{@@printindex} produces links to the index entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8335
msgid ""
"In DocBook and @LaTeX{} output, it simply records the index to be printed."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8338
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8340
#, no-wrap
msgid "Combining Indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8340
msgid "@nodedescription How to combine indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8341
#, no-wrap
msgid "Combining indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8342
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indices, combining them"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8347
msgid ""
"Sometimes you will want to combine two disparate indices such as functions "
"and concepts, perhaps because you have few enough entries that a separate "
"index would look silly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8360
msgid ""
"You could put functions into the concept index by writing @code{@@cindex} "
"commands for them instead of @code{@@findex} commands, and produce a "
"consistent manual by printing the concept index with the title `Function and "
"Concept Index' and not printing the `Function Index' at all; but this is not "
"a robust procedure.  It works only if your document is never included as "
"part of another document that is designed to have a separate function index; "
"if your document were to be included with such a document, the functions "
"from your document and those from the other would not end up together.  "
"Also, to make your function names appear in the right font in the concept "
"index, you would need to enclose every one of them between the braces of "
"@code{@@code}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8362
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@syncodeindex}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8364
msgid ""
"@nodedescription How to merge two indices, using @code{@@code} font for the "
"merged-from index."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8364
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@syncodeindex}: Combining Indices Using @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8367
#, no-wrap
msgid "syncodeindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8373
msgid ""
"When you want to combine functions and concepts into one index, you should "
"index the functions with @code{@@findex} and index the concepts with "
"@code{@@cindex}, and use the @code{@@syncodeindex} command to redirect the "
"function index entries into the concept index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8377
msgid ""
"The @code{@@syncodeindex} command takes two arguments; they are the name of "
"the index to redirect, and the name of the index to redirect it to.  The "
"template looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8380
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@syncodeindex @var{from} @var{to}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8382
#, no-wrap
msgid "Predefined names for indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8383
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two letter names for indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8384
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indices, two letter names"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8385
#, no-wrap
msgid "Names for indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8387
msgid "For this purpose, the indices are given two-letter names:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8391
msgid "Concept index"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8393
msgid "Function index"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8395
msgid "Key index"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8397
msgid "Program index"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8399
msgid "Data type index"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8401
msgid "Variable index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8407
msgid ""
"Write a @code{@@syncodeindex} command before or shortly after the "
"end-of-header line at the beginning of a Texinfo file.  For example, to "
"merge a function index with a concept index, write the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8410
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@syncodeindex fn cp\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8415
msgid ""
"This causes all entries designated for the function index to merge in with "
"the concept index instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8418
msgid ""
"To merge both a variable index and a function index into a concept index, "
"write the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8423
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@syncodeindex vr cp\n"
"@@syncodeindex fn cp\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8426
#, no-wrap
msgid "Fonts for indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8433
msgid ""
"The @code{@@syncodeindex} command puts all the entries from the `from' index "
"(the redirected index) into the @code{@@code} font, overriding whatever "
"default font is used by the index to which the entries are now directed.  "
"This way, if you direct function names from a function index into a concept "
"index, all the function names are printed in the @code{@@code} font as you "
"would expect."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8435
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@synindex}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8437
msgid ""
"@nodedescription How to merge two indices, using the roman font for the "
"merged-from index."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8437
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@synindex}: Combining Indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8440
#, no-wrap
msgid "synindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8447
msgid ""
"The @code{@@synindex} command is nearly the same as the "
"@code{@@syncodeindex} command, except that it does not put the `from' index "
"entries into the @code{@@code} font; rather it puts them in the roman font.  "
"Thus, you use @code{@@synindex} when you merge a concept index into a "
"function index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8450
msgid ""
"@xref{Printing Indices & Menus}, for information about printing an index at "
"the end of a book or creating an index menu in an Info file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8452
#, no-wrap
msgid "New Indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8454
msgid "@nodedescription How to define your own indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8454
#, no-wrap
msgid "Defining New Indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8456
#, no-wrap
msgid "Defining new indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8457
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indices, defining new"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8458
#, no-wrap
msgid "New index defining"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8459
#, no-wrap
msgid "defindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8460
#, no-wrap
msgid "defcodeindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8467
msgid ""
"In addition to the predefined indices (@pxref{Predefined Indices}), you may "
"use the @code{@@defindex} and @code{@@defcodeindex} commands to define new "
"indices.  These commands create new indexing @@-commands with which you mark "
"index entries.  The @code{@@defindex} command is used like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8470
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defindex @var{name}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8474
msgid ""
"New index names are usually two-letter words, such as @samp{au}.  For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8477
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defindex au\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8483
msgid ""
"This defines a new index, called the @samp{au} index.  At the same time, it "
"creates a new indexing command, @code{@@auindex}, that you can use to make "
"index entries.  Use this new indexing command just as you would use a "
"predefined indexing command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8486
msgid ""
"For example, here is a section heading followed by a concept index entry and "
"two @samp{au} index entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8492
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@section Cognitive Semantics\n"
"@@cindex kinesthetic image schemas\n"
"@@auindex Johnson, Mark\n"
"@@auindex Lakoff, George\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8496
msgid "(Evidently, @samp{au} serves here as an abbreviation for ``author''.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8500
msgid ""
"Texinfo constructs the new indexing command by concatenating the name of the "
"index with @samp{index}; thus, defining an @samp{xy} index leads to the "
"automatic creation of an @code{@@xyindex} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8503
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@printindex} command to print the index, as you do with the "
"predefined indices.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8508
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Author Index\n"
"@@unnumbered Author Index\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8510
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@printindex au\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8516
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defcodeindex} command is like the @code{@@defindex} command, "
"except that, in the printed output, it prints entries in an @code{@@code} "
"font by default instead of in a roman font."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8520
msgid ""
"You should define new indices before the end-of-header line of a Texinfo "
"file, and (of course) before any @code{@@synindex} or @code{@@syncodeindex} "
"commands (@pxref{Texinfo File Header})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8525
msgid ""
"As mentioned earlier (@pxref{Predefined Indices}), we recommend having a "
"single index in the final document whenever possible (no matter how many "
"source indices you use), since then readers have only one place to look."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8532
msgid ""
"When creating an index, @TeX{} creates a file whose extension is the name of "
"the index (@pxref{Names of index files}).  Therefore you should avoid using "
"index names that collide with extensions used for other purposes, such as "
"@samp{.aux} or @samp{.xml}.  @command{texi2any} already reports an error if "
"a new index conflicts well-known extension name."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8534
#, no-wrap
msgid "Insertions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8536
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting @@-signs, braces, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8536
#, no-wrap
msgid "Special Insertions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8537
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting special characters and symbols"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8538
#, no-wrap
msgid "Special insertions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8543
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides several commands for inserting characters that have special "
"meaning in Texinfo, such as braces, and for other graphic elements that do "
"not correspond to simple characters you can type."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8545
#, no-wrap
msgid "Special Characters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8547
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting @@ @{@} , \\ # &"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8547
#, no-wrap
msgid "Special Characters: Inserting @@ @{@} , \\ # &"
msgstr ""

#.  previous names for this node
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8550
msgid "Braces Atsign"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8550
msgid "Atsign Braces Comma"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8550
#, no-wrap
msgid "Special characters, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8551
#, no-wrap
msgid "Commands to insert special characters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8557
msgid ""
"@samp{@@} and curly braces are the basic special characters in Texinfo.  To "
"insert these characters so they appear in text, you must put an @samp{@@} in "
"front of these characters to prevent Texinfo from misinterpreting them.  "
"Alphabetic commands are also provided."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8560
msgid ""
"The other characters (comma, backslash, hash, ampersand) are special only in "
"restricted contexts, as explained in the respective sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8562
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting an Atsign"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8564
msgid "@nodedescription @code{@@@@}, @code{@@atchar@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8564
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting `@@' with @code{@@@@} and @code{@@atchar@{@}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8565
#, no-wrap
msgid "At sign, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8566
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting @@ @r{(literal @samp{@@})}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8567
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{@@} @@ @r{(literal @samp{@@})}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8569
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{@@} @@"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8571
#, no-wrap
msgid "atchar"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8575
msgid ""
"@code{@@@@} produces a single @samp{@@} character in the output.  Do not put "
"braces after an @code{@@@@} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8581
msgid ""
"@code{@@atchar@{@}} also produces a single @samp{@@} character in the "
"output.  It does need following braces, as usual for alphabetic commands.  "
"In inline conditionals (@pxref{Inline Conditionals}), it can be necessary to "
"avoid using the literal @samp{@@} character in the source (and may be "
"clearer in other contexts)."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8583
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting Braces"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8585
msgid "@nodedescription @code{@@@{ @@@}}, @code{@@l rbracechar@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8585
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting `@{ `@}' with @code{@@@{ @@@}} and @code{@@l rbracechar@{@}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8587
#, no-wrap
msgid "@{ @r{(literal @samp{@{})}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8588
#, no-wrap
msgid "@} @r{(literal @samp{@}})}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8590
#, no-wrap
msgid "@{"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8591
#, no-wrap
msgid "@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8593
#, no-wrap
msgid "lbracechar"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8594
#, no-wrap
msgid "rbracechar"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8595
#, no-wrap
msgid "Braces, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8600
msgid ""
"@code{@@@{} produces a single @samp{@{} in the output, and @code{@@@}} "
"produces a single @samp{@}}.  Do not put braces after either an @code{@@@{} "
"or an @code{@@@}} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8607
msgid ""
"@code{@@lbracechar@{@}} and @code{@@rbracechar@{@}} also produce single "
"@samp{@{} and @samp{@}} characters in the output.  They do need following "
"braces, as usual for alphabetic commands.  In inline conditionals "
"(@pxref{Inline Conditionals}), it can be necessary to avoid using literal "
"brace characters in the source (and may be clearer in other contexts)."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8609
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting a Comma"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8611
msgid "@nodedescription , and @code{@@comma@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8611
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting `,' with @code{@@comma@{@}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8613
#, no-wrap
msgid "comma"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8614
#, no-wrap
msgid "Comma, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8618
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, a comma `,' is a normal character that can be simply typed in "
"your input where you need it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8625
msgid ""
"However, Texinfo uses the comma as a special character only in one context: "
"to separate arguments to those Texinfo commands, such as @code{@@node} "
"(@pxref{Node Line Requirements}), @code{@@acronym} "
"(@pxref{@code{@@acronym}}) and @code{@@xref} (@pxref{Cross References}), as "
"well as user-defined macros (@pxref{Defining Macros}), which take more than "
"one argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8629
msgid ""
"Since a comma character would confuse Texinfo's parsing for these commands, "
"you must use the command @samp{@@comma@{@}} instead if you want to pass an "
"actual comma.  Here are some examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8634
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@acronym@{ABC, A Bizarre @@comma@{@}@}\n"
"@@xref@{Comma,, The @@comma@{@} symbol@}\n"
"@@mymac@{One argument@@comma@{@} containing a comma@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8638
msgid ""
"Although @samp{@@comma@{@}} can be used nearly anywhere, there is no need "
"for it anywhere except in this unusual case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8642
msgid ""
"(Incidentally, the name @samp{@@comma} lacks the @samp{char} suffix used in "
"its companion commands only for historical reasons.  It didn't seem "
"important enough to define a synonym.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8644
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting a Backslash"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8646
msgid "@nodedescription \\ and @code{@@backslashchar@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8646
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting `\\' with @code{@@backslashchar@{@}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8648
#, no-wrap
msgid "backslashchar"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8649
#, no-wrap
msgid "Backslash, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8654
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, a backslash `\\' is a normal character in Texinfo that can be "
"simply typed in your input where you need it.  The result is to typeset the "
"backslash from the typewriter font."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8658
msgid ""
"However, Texinfo uses the backslash as a special character in one restricted "
"context: to delimit formal arguments in the bodies of user-defined macros "
"(@pxref{Defining Macros})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8663
msgid ""
"Due to the vagaries of macro argument parsing, it is more reliable to pass "
"an alphabetic command that produces a backslash instead of using a literal "
"\\.  Hence @code{@@backslashchar@{@}}.  Here is an example macro call:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8666
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@mymac@{One argument@@backslashchar@{@} with a backslash@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8668
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\backslash"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8669
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{\\} \\ @r{(literal @samp{\\} in math)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8671
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{\\} \\"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8678
msgid ""
"Texinfo documents may also use \\ as a command character inside "
"@code{@@math} (@pxref{Inserting Math}).  In this case, @code{@@\\} or "
"@code{\\backslash} produce a ``math'' backslash (from the math symbol font), "
"while @code{@@backslashchar@{@}} produces a typewriter backslash as usual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8681
msgid ""
"Although @samp{@@backslashchar@{@}} can be used nearly anywhere, there is no "
"need for it except in these unusual cases."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8683
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting a Hashsign"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8685
msgid "@nodedescription # and @code{@@hashchar@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8685
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting `#' with @code{@@hashchar@{@}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8687
#, no-wrap
msgid "hashchar"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8688
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting #"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8689
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hash sign, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8694
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, a hash `#' is a normal character in Texinfo that can be simply "
"typed in your input where you need it.  The result is to typeset the hash "
"character from the current font."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8695
#, no-wrap
msgid "Number sign, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8696
#, no-wrap
msgid "Octotherp, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8697
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sharp sign (not), inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8703
msgid ""
"This character has many other names, varying by locale, such as ``number "
"sign'', ``pound'', and ``octothorp''.  It is also sometimes called ``sharp'' "
"or ``sharp sign'' since it vaguely resembles the musical symbol by that "
"name.  In situations where Texinfo is used, ``hash'' is the most common in "
"our experience."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8707
msgid ""
"However, Texinfo uses the hash character as a special character in one "
"restricted context: to introduce the so-called @code{#line} directive and "
"variants (@pxref{External Macro Processors})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8712
msgid ""
"So, in order to typeset an actual hash character in such a place (for "
"example, in a program that needs documentation about @code{#line}), it's "
"necessary to use @code{@@hashchar@{@}} or some other construct.  Here's an "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8715
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@hashchar@{@} 10 \"example.c\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8719
msgid ""
"Although @samp{@@hashchar@{@}} can be used nearly anywhere, there is no need "
"for it anywhere except this unusual case."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8721
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting an Ampersand"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8723
msgid "@nodedescription & and @code{@@ampchar@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8723
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting `&' with @code{@@&} and @code{@@ampchar@{@}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8725
#, no-wrap
msgid "ampchar"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8726
#, no-wrap
msgid "& @r{(literal @samp{&})}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8728
#, no-wrap
msgid "&"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8730
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting &"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8731
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ampersand, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8736
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, an ampersand `&' is a normal character in Texinfo that can be "
"simply typed in your input where you need it.  The result is to typeset the "
"ampersand character."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8744
msgid ""
"However, the ampersand character is treated specially in just one restricted "
"context.  In the argument to a definition command (@pxref{Definition "
"Commands}), an ampersand followed by a series of letters may be typeset "
"specially when processing with @TeX{} @footnote{This syntax is used for "
"Emacs Lisp keywords. @xref{A Sample Function Description,,, elisp, GNU Emacs "
"Lisp Reference Manual}.} (@pxref{Def Cmd Conventions}).  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8749
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defun foo integer1 &optional integer2 &rest integers\n"
"@@code@{foo@} described here.\n"
"@@end defun\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8753
msgid "even without any @@-command involved."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8757
msgid ""
"So, in order to typeset an ampersand in such a context, it's necessary to "
"use @code{@@&} or some other construct.  Here's an example of a C++ function "
"taking a reference as a parameter:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8762
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftypefn Function int foo (const std::vector<int>@@& @@var@{bar@})\n"
"Documentation of @@code@{foo@}.\n"
"@@end deftypefn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8766
#, no-wrap
msgid "Function int foo (const std::vector<int>@& @var{bar})"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8768
msgid "Documentation of @code{foo}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8772
msgid ""
"Although @samp{@@&} and @samp{@@ampchar@{@}} can be used nearly anywhere, "
"there is no need for them anywhere except this unusual case."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8774
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8776
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting Quote Characters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8776
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting left and right quotes, in code."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8778
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting quote characters"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8779
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quote characters, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8787
msgid ""
"As explained in the early section on general Texinfo input conventions "
"(@pxref{Conventions}), Texinfo source files use the ASCII character @code{`} "
"(96 decimal) to produce a left quote (`), and ASCII @code{'} (39 decimal) to "
"produce a right quote (').  Doubling these input characters (@code{``} and "
"@code{''}) produces double quotes (`` and '').  These are the conventions "
"used by @TeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8792
msgid ""
"In examples of computer code, however, @code{`} and @code{'} produce typical "
"renderings for these ASCII characters: the backtick character (standalone "
"grave accent) and undirected single quote respectively.  In the past, "
"directed glyphs"
msgstr ""

#. type: iftex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8796
msgid "(@code{`} and @code{'})"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8801
msgid ""
"were used by default in @TeX{} output.  Texinfo provides these commands to "
"choose between these alternate renderings:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8803
#, no-wrap
msgid "codequoteundirected"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8804
#, no-wrap
msgid "undirected single quote"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8805
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18724
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@codequoteundirected @var{on-off}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8808
msgid ""
"Set to @samp{off} to output the @code{'} character in code environments as "
"the right curly single quote."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8809
#, no-wrap
msgid "codequotebacktick"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8810
#, no-wrap
msgid "Backtick"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8811
#, no-wrap
msgid "Grave accent @subentry standalone"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8812
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18723
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@codequotebacktick @var{on-off}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8815
msgid ""
"Set to @samp{off} to output the @code{`} character in code environments as "
"the left curly single quote."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8820
msgid ""
"If you want these settings for only part of the document, "
"@code{@@codequote... on} will restore the normal behavior, as in "
"@code{@@codequoteundirected on}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8824
msgid ""
"These settings affect @code{@@code}, @code{@@example}, @code{@@kbd}, "
"@code{@@samp}, @code{@@verb}, and @code{@@verbatim}.  @xref{Useful "
"Highlighting}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8829
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, some document viewers will mangle the directed quote "
"characters when copying and pasting.  (The free PDF reader @command{xpdf} "
"works fine, but other PDF readers, both free and nonfree, have problems.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8830
#, no-wrap
msgid "txicodequoteundirected"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8831
#, no-wrap
msgid "txicodequotebacktick"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8835
msgid ""
"This feature can also be controlled by using @code{@@set} and @code{@@clear} "
"on the corresponding variables @code{txicodequoteundirected} and "
"@code{txicodequotebacktick}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8837
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8839
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting Space"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8839
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting the right amount of whitespace."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8841
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting space"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8842
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spacing, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8845
msgid ""
"The following sections describe commands that control spacing of various "
"kinds within and after sentences."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8847
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8849
#, no-wrap
msgid "Multiple Spaces"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8849
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting multiple spaces."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8851
#, no-wrap
msgid "Multiple spaces"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8852
#, no-wrap
msgid "Whitespace, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8853
#, no-wrap
msgid "Space, inserting horizontal"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8854
#, no-wrap
msgid "<space>"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8855
#, no-wrap
msgid "<tab>"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8856
#, no-wrap
msgid "<newline>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8860
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, multiple whitespace characters (space, tab, and newline)  are "
"collapsed into a single space."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8871
msgid ""
"Occasionally, you may want to produce several consecutive spaces, either for "
"purposes of example (e.g., what your program does with multiple spaces as "
"input), or merely for purposes of appearance in headings or lists.  Texinfo "
"supports three commands: @code{@@@kbd{SPACE}}, @code{@@@kbd{TAB}}, and "
"@code{@@@kbd{NL}}, all of which insert a single space into the output.  "
"(Here, @code{@@@kbd{SPACE}} represents an @samp{@@} character followed by a "
"space, i.e., @samp{@@ }, @kbd{TAB} represents an actual tab character, and "
"@code{@@@kbd{NL}} represents an @samp{@@} character and end-of-line, i.e., "
"when @samp{@@} is the last character on a line.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8876
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Spacey@@ @@ @@ @@\n"
"example.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8883
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Spacey@ @ @ @\n"
"example.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8886
msgid "Do not follow any of these commands with braces."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8888
msgid "To produce a non-breakable space, see @ref{@code{@@tie}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8890
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8892
#, no-wrap
msgid "Not Ending a Sentence"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8892
msgid "@nodedescription Sometimes a .@ doesn't end a sentence."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8894
#, no-wrap
msgid "Not ending a sentence"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8895
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sentence non-ending punctuation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8896
#, no-wrap
msgid "Periods, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8897
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spacing, in the middle of sentences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8901
msgid ""
"When a period, exclamation point or question mark is at the end of a "
"sentence, slightly more space is inserted after it in a typeset manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8902
#, no-wrap
msgid ": @r{(suppress end-of-sentence space)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8904
#, no-wrap
msgid ":"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8911
msgid ""
"Usually, Texinfo can determine automatically when a period ends a sentence.  "
"However, special commands are needed in some circumstances.  Use the "
"@code{@@:} command after a period, question mark, exclamation mark or colon "
"that should not be followed by extra space.  This is necessary in the "
"following situations:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8913
#, no-wrap
msgid "After a period that ends a lowercase abbreviation which is not at"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8915
msgid "the end of a sentence."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8916
#, no-wrap
msgid "When a parenthetical remark in the middle of a sentence (like"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8920
msgid ""
"this one!)@: ends with a period, exclamation point or question mark, "
"@code{@@:} should be used after the right parenthesis.  Similarly for right "
"brackets and right quotes (both single and double)."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8926
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{foo vs.@@: bar (or?)@@: baz},\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8931
msgid ""
"The first line below shows the output, and for comparison, the second line "
"shows the spacing when the @samp{@@:} commands were not used."
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8935
msgid "foo vs.@: bar (or?)@: baz@* foo vs. bar (or?) baz"
msgstr ""

#. type: iftex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8940
msgid ""
"If you look carefully, you will see a bit of extraneous space after the "
"@samp{vs.}@: and @samp{(or?)}@:."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8945
msgid ""
"It may help you to remember what @code{@@:} does by imagining that it stands "
"for an invisible lower-case character that stops a word ending in a period."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8952
msgid ""
"A few Texinfo commands force normal interword spacing, so that you don't "
"have to insert @code{@@:} where you otherwise would.  These are the "
"code-like highlighting commands, @code{@@var}, @code{@@abbr}, and "
"@code{@@acronym} (@pxref{Useful Highlighting}).  For example, in "
"@samp{@@code@{foo. bar@}} the period is not considered to be the end of a "
"sentence, and no extra space is inserted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8954
msgid "@code{@@:} has no effect on the HTML or DocBook output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8956
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8958
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8960
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ending a Sentence"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8958
msgid "@nodedescription Sometimes it does."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8961
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sentence ending punctuation"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8963
#, no-wrap
msgid ". @r{(end of sentence)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8964
#, no-wrap
msgid "! @r{(end of sentence)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8965
#, no-wrap
msgid "? @r{(end of sentence)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8967
#, no-wrap
msgid "."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8968
#, no-wrap
msgid "!"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8969
#, no-wrap
msgid "?"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8971
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spacing, at ends of sentences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8977
msgid ""
"As mentioned above, Texinfo normally inserts additional space after the end "
"of a sentence.  It uses the same heuristic for this as @TeX{}: a sentence "
"ends with a period, exclamation point, or question mark, either preceded or "
"followed by optional closing punctuation, and then whitespace, and "
"@emph{not} preceded by a capital letter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8982
msgid ""
"Use @code{@@.}@: instead of a period, @code{@@!}@: instead of an exclamation "
"point, and @code{@@?}@: instead of a question mark at the end of a sentence "
"that does end with a capital letter.  Do not put braces after any of these "
"commands.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8986
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Give it to M.I.B. and to M.E.W@@.  Also, give it to R.J.C@@.\n"
"Give it to M.I.B. and to M.E.W.  Also, give it to R.J.C.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8991
msgid ""
"The output follows.  In printed output and Info, you can see the desired "
"extra whitespace after the @samp{W} in the first line."
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8995
msgid ""
"Give it to M.I.B. and to M.E.W@.  Also, give it to R.J.C@.@* Give it to "
"M.I.B. and to M.E.W.  Also, give it to R.J.C."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:8999
msgid ""
"In other output formats, @code{@@.}@: is equivalent to a simple @samp{.}; "
"likewise for @code{@@!}@: and @code{@@?}@:."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9000
#, no-wrap
msgid "Closing punctuation, and sentence ending"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9009
msgid ""
"The ``closing punctuation'' mentioned above is defined as a right "
"parenthesis (@samp{)}, right bracket (@samp{]}), or right quote, either "
"single or double (@samp{'} and @samp{''}; the many possible additional "
"Unicode right quotes are not included).  These characters can be thought of "
"as invisible with respect to whether a given period ends a sentence.  (This "
"is the same rule as @TeX{}.)  For instance, the periods in @samp{foo.) Bar} "
"and @samp{foo.'' Bar} do end sentences."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9017
msgid ""
"The meanings of @code{@@:} and @code{@@.}, etc.@: in Texinfo are designed to "
"work well with the Emacs sentence motion commands (@pxref{Sentences,,, "
"emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}).  It may help to imagine that the @samp{@@} in "
"@samp{@@.}, etc., is an invisible lower-case letter `a' which makes an "
"upper-case letter before it immaterial for the purposes of deciding whether "
"the period ends the sentence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9027
msgid ""
"A few Texinfo commands are not considered as being an abbreviation, even "
"though they may end with a capital letter when expanded, so that you don't "
"have to insert @code{@@.} and companions.  Notably, this is the case for "
"code-like highlighting commands, @code{@@var} arguments ending with a "
"capital letter, @code{@@LaTeX}, and @code{@@TeX}.  For example, that "
"sentence ended with @samp{... @@code@{@@@@TeX@}.}; @code{@@.} was not "
"needed.  Similarly, in @code{... @@var@{VARNAME@}. Text} the period after "
"@var{VARNAME} ends the sentence; there is no need to use @code{@@.}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9029
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@frenchspacing}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9031
msgid "@nodedescription Specifying end-of-sentence spacing."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9031
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@frenchspacing} @var{val}: Control Sentence Spacing"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9034
#, no-wrap
msgid "frenchspacing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9035
#, no-wrap
msgid "French spacing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9036
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sentences, spacing after"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9037
#, no-wrap
msgid "Space, after sentences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9044
msgid ""
"In American typography, it is traditional and correct to put extra space at "
"the end of a sentence.  This is the default in Texinfo (implemented in Info "
"and printed output, not in other output formats).  In French typography (and "
"others), this extra space is wrong; all spaces are uniform."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9050
msgid ""
"Therefore Texinfo provides the @code{@@frenchspacing} command to control the "
"spacing after punctuation.  It reads the rest of the line as its argument, "
"which must be the single word @samp{on} or @samp{off} (always these words, "
"regardless of the language of the document).  Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9054
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@frenchspacing on\n"
"This is text. Two sentences. Three sentences. French spacing.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9057
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@frenchspacing off\n"
"This is text. Two sentences. Three sentences. Non-French spacing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9063
msgid ""
"@frenchspacing on This is text. Two sentences. Three sentences. French "
"spacing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9066
msgid ""
"@frenchspacing off This is text. Two sentences. Three sentences. Non-French "
"spacing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9069
msgid ""
"@code{@@frenchspacing} also affects the output after @code{@@.}, @code{@@!}, "
"and @code{@@?} (@pxref{Ending a Sentence})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9071
msgid "@code{@@frenchspacing} has no effect on the HTML or DocBook output."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9073
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@dmn}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9075
msgid "@nodedescription Formatting a dimension."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9075
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@dmn}@{@var{dimension}@}: Format a Dimension"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9078
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9081
#, no-wrap
msgid "dmn"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9078
#, no-wrap
msgid "Thin space between number, dimension"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9079
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dimension formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9080
#, no-wrap
msgid "Format a dimension"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9086
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{@@dmn} command to format a dimension with just enough "
"space for proper typesetting inserted in printed output.  In other output "
"formats, the formatting commands insert no space at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9090
msgid ""
"To use the @code{@@dmn} command, write the number and then follow it "
"immediately, with no intervening space, by @code{@@dmn}, and then by the "
"dimension within braces.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9093
#, no-wrap
msgid "A4 paper is 8.27@@dmn@{in@} wide.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9100
msgid "A4 paper is 8.27@dmn{in} wide."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9110
msgid ""
"Not everyone uses this style.  Some people prefer `8.27@tie{}in.'@: or "
"`8.27@tie{}inches'.  In these cases, however, you need to use @code{@@tie} "
"(@pxref{@code{@@tie}}) or @code{@@w} (@pxref{@code{@@w}})  so that no line "
"break can occur between the number and the dimension.  Also, if you write a "
"period after an abbreviation within a sentence (as with the `in.'@: above), "
"you should write @samp{@@:} after the period to avoid inserting extra "
"whitespace in printed output, as shown here.  @xref{Not Ending a Sentence}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9112
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9114
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting Accents"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9114
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting accents and special characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9116
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting accents"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9117
#, no-wrap
msgid "Accents, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9118
#, no-wrap
msgid "Floating accents, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9127
msgid ""
"Here is a table with the commands Texinfo provides for inserting floating "
"accents.  They all need an argument, the character to accent, which can "
"either be given in braces as usual (@code{@@'@{e@}}), or, as a special case, "
"the braces can be omitted, in which case the argument is the next character "
"(@code{@@'e}).  This is to make the source as convenient as possible to type "
"and read, since accented characters are very common in some languages."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9133
msgid ""
"If the command is alphabetic, such as @code{@@dotaccent}, then there must be "
"a space between the command name and argument if braces are not used.  If "
"the command is non-alphabetic, such as @code{@@'}, then there must "
"@emph{not} be a space; the argument is the very next character."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9136
msgid ""
"Exception: the argument to @code{@@tieaccent} must be enclosed in braces "
"(since it is two characters instead of one)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9142
msgid ""
"In Info and plain text output, accent constructs are output as the true "
"accented characters if the document encoding supports the required "
"characters, unless the option @option{--disable-encoding} is given to "
"@command{texi2any} (@pxref{@code{@@documentencoding}}).  ASCII "
"transliterations are used if the encoded characters are not output."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9143
#, no-wrap
msgid "\" @r{(umlaut accent)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9144
#, no-wrap
msgid "Umlaut accent"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9145
#, no-wrap
msgid "' @r{(acute accent)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9146
#, no-wrap
msgid "Acute accent"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9147
#, no-wrap
msgid "= @r{(macron accent)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:9148
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macron accent"
