#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17307
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tag and Split Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17308
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tag Files and Split Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17310
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tag table"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17311
#, no-wrap
msgid "nonsplit Info file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17312
#, no-wrap
msgid "split Info file"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17316
msgid ""
"Info files always contain a @dfn{tag table}, to be able to jump to nodes "
"quickly.  Info files can be @dfn{nonsplit} (also called @dfn{unsplit}) or "
"@dfn{split}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17317
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indirect subfiles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17327
msgid ""
"If the Info file contains less than about 300,000 characters the file should "
"be nonsplit.  In that case, the tag table should appear at the end of the "
"Info file.  If the Texinfo file contains more than about 300,000 characters, "
"Texinfo processors split the large Info file into shorter @dfn{indirect} "
"subfiles of about 300,000 characters each.  With @command{texi2any}, "
"splitting may be prevented by @option{--no-split}, and the default size of "
"300,000 characters may be modified with @option{--split-size} "
"(@pxref{Invoking @command{texi2any}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17332
msgid ""
"When a file is split, Info itself makes use of a shortened version of the "
"original file that contains just the tag table and references to the files "
"that were split off.  The split-off files are called @dfn{indirect} files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17338
msgid ""
"The split-off files have names that are created by appending @w{@samp{-1}}, "
"@w{@samp{-2}}, @w{@samp{-3}} and so on to the output file name, specified by "
"the @code{@@setfilename} command or the input file name.  The shortened "
"version of the original file continues to have the name specified by "
"@code{@@setfilename} or the input file name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17341
msgid ""
"At one stage in writing this document, for example, the Info file was saved "
"as the file @file{test-texinfo} and that file looked like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17347
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Info file: test-texinfo,    -*-Text-*-\n"
"produced by texinfo-format-buffer\n"
"from file: new-texinfo-manual.texinfo\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17352
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"^_\n"
"Indirect:\n"
"test-texinfo-1: 102\n"
"test-texinfo-2: 50422\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17360
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"test-texinfo-3: 101300\n"
"^_^L\n"
"Tag table:\n"
"(Indirect)\n"
"Node: overview^?104\n"
"Node: info file^?1271\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17365
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Node: printed manual^?4853\n"
"Node: conventions^?6855\n"
"@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17374
msgid ""
"(But @file{test-texinfo} had far more nodes than are shown here.)  Each of "
"the split-off, indirect files, @file{test-texinfo-1}, @file{test-texinfo-2}, "
"and @file{test-texinfo-3}, is listed in this file after the line that says "
"@samp{Indirect:}.  The tag table is listed after the line that says "
"@samp{Tag table:}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17381
msgid ""
"In the list of indirect files, the number following the file name records "
"the cumulative number of bytes in the preceding indirect files, not counting "
"the file list itself, the tag table, or any permissions text in the first "
"file.  In the tag table, the number following the node name records the "
"location of the beginning of the node, in bytes from the beginning of the "
"(unsplit) output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17390
msgid ""
"If you are using @code{texinfo-format-buffer} to create Info files, you may "
"want to run the @code{Info-validate} command.  (The @command{texi2any} "
"command does such a good job on its own, you do not need "
"@code{Info-validate}.)  However, you cannot run the @kbd{M-x Info-validate} "
"node-checking command on indirect files.  For information on how to prevent "
"files from being split with @code{texinfo-format-buffer} and how to validate "
"the structure of the nodes, see @ref{Using @code{Info-validate}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17392
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17393
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format FAQ"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17397
msgid ""
"Here are some questions that have been frequently asked on the project "
"mailing lists and elsewhere."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17399
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Why when I run @samp{info @var{foo}} do I get the same output as @samp{man "
"@var{foo}}?"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17405
msgid ""
"Check that the Info manuals are installed.  Not all GNU/Linux distributions "
"install all the Info manuals by default.  This is regrettable, as often the "
"Info manual provides more information than the provided man page."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17406
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why not use HTML instead of Info?"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17415
msgid ""
"Manuals are rarely written in the Info format itself, but are generated from "
"Texinfo source by the @command{texi2any} program.  @command{texi2any} can "
"generate HTML as well as Info from Texinfo source. You can also access and "
"download HTML manuals from the GNU website "
"(@uref{https://www.gnu.org/manual/manual.html}).  Additionally, some "
"GNU/Linux distributions provide packages for the installation of HTML "
"manuals."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17424
msgid ""
"Info still has some advantages over HTML for locally installed "
"documentation.  The Info browsers support index lookup and support for links "
"between locally installed manuals in multiple locations.  It's important to "
"have documentation installed locally on your machine rather than relying on "
"the Internet; this makes accessing documentation more reliable, and ensures "
"it matches installed versions of packages.  It's hoped that support for "
"browsing locally installed Texinfo documentation in some form of HTML can be "
"improved in the future."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17426
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Why provide the command-line @command{info} program when the Emacs Info "
"reader is better?"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17437
msgid ""
"The Emacs Info reader can display images, and fully supports using a mouse.  "
"There are not many differences among the Info readers besides that.  "
"Command-line @command{info} can be configured to change the default key "
"bindings, as well as change the color of cross-references and search "
"results, and to enable mouse scrolling support.  You should not need to be "
"experienced with the Emacs editor to use @command{info}.  (Some recommend "
"another program called @command{pinfo}, but this lacks in important features "
"like index lookup.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17445
msgid ""
"Some prefer to be able to scroll through the entire manual at once (as "
"happens with man pages), rather than being limited to a single `node' of "
"content at once.  Of course, there is no accounting for taste, but a single, "
"unstructured block of text becomes more awkward as a manual becomes longer "
"and more complicated.  (However, if you really want to, you can view an info "
"manual all at once in the @command{less} pager with @samp{info @var{foo} | "
"less}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17446
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why do I have `see' appearing in front of all of my links?"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17454
msgid ""
"By default, Emacs Info mode either changes the marker @samp{*note} for "
"cross-references to `see', or hides it completely.  Command-line "
"@command{info} does the same if @code{hide-note-references} is set.  "
"Unfortunately, there is no way to do this reliably, as both the "
"@code{@@pxref} and @code{@@ref} commands in Texinfo output this marker in "
"the Info output, and the `see' text is only appropriate for @code{@@pxref}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17455
#, no-wrap
msgid "Yes, but how do I get a plain link, with no extra markup?"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17460
msgid ""
"You can't.  Info is a plain text format that is displayed mostly as-is in "
"the viewers, and without the @samp{*note} text there would be nothing to "
"mark text as a link."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17465
msgid ""
"For output formats such as HTML, you can use the @code{@@link} command to "
"produce a plain link.  @xref{@code{@@link}}.  This does not produce a "
"working cross-reference in Info output or in a printed copy of the manual, "
"though."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17467
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why do lines containing links appear mysteriously short?"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17471
msgid ""
"This due to Emacs (or @command{info} with @code{hide-note-references} set to "
"@samp{On}) hiding @samp{*note} strings, as mentioned above."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17472
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Can the Info format be extended to support fonts, colors or reflowable text?"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17477
msgid ""
"Any extension would be incompatible with existing Info-viewing programs.  "
"Extra markup added to Info files would be displayed to the user, making "
"files ugly and unreadable."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17483
msgid ""
"When Texinfo files are processed into Info files, information about which "
"Texinfo commands were used to markup text is lost.  Moreover, it is not "
"possible to reliably detect whether text can be reflowed (e.g.@: a paragraph "
"of prose), or whether line breaks should be kept (e.g.@: a code sample, or "
"poem)."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17487
msgid ""
"Info's core purpose is to display documentation on text terminals.  If you "
"want more, you are recommended to use the HTML output from "
"@command{texi2any} instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17491
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17493
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17495
#, no-wrap
msgid "Generating HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17493
msgid "@nodedescription Details on HTML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17496
#, no-wrap
msgid "Outputting HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17501
msgid ""
"@command{texi2any} generates Info output by default, but given the "
"@option{--html} option, it will generate HTML, for web browsers and other "
"programs.  This chapter gives some details on such HTML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17509
msgid ""
"@command{texi2any} has many user-definable customization variables with "
"which you can influence the HTML output.  @xref{Customization Variables}.  "
"In particular, there is support for syntax highlighting in @code{@@example} "
"(@pxref{Syntax Highlighting}). You can also write so-called "
"@dfn{initialization files}, or @dfn{init files} for short, to modify almost "
"every aspect of HTML output formatting.  Initialization files contain code "
"and are loaded by @option{--init-file} (@pxref{Invoking @command{texi2any}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17514
msgid ""
"Some initialization files are maintained with Texinfo and installed in the "
"default case.  For example, @file{chm.pm} produces the intermediate "
"compressed HTML Help format files that can be subsequently converted to the "
"CHM format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17518
msgid ""
"The documentation of @command{texi2any} HTML output adaptation using "
"customization files is in a separate manual.  @xref{,,, texi2any_api, GNU "
"Texinfo @command{texi2any} Output Customization}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17520
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17522
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Translation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17522
msgid "@nodedescription Details of the HTML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17524
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML translation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17529
msgid ""
"The HTML generated by @command{texi2any} generates standard HTML output.  "
"The output is intentionally quite plain for maximum portability and "
"accessibility."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17533
msgid ""
"You can customize the output via CSS (@pxref{HTML CSS}) or other means "
"(@pxref{Customization Variables}).  If you cannot accomplish a reasonable "
"customization, feel free to report that."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17534
#, no-wrap
msgid "Navigation bar, in HTML output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17541
msgid ""
"@strong{Navigation bar:} By default, a navigation bar is inserted at the "
"start of each node, analogous to Info output.  If the @samp{--no-headers} "
"option is used, the navigation bar is only inserted at the beginning of "
"split files.  Header @code{<link>} elements in split output support "
"Info-like navigation with browsers which implement this feature."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17542
#, no-wrap
msgid "Escaping to HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17543
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raw HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17551
msgid ""
"@strong{Raw HTML}: @command{texi2any} will include segments of Texinfo "
"source between @code{@@ifhtml} and @code{@@end ifhtml} in the HTML output "
"(but not any of the other conditionals, by default).  Source between "
"@code{@@html} and @code{@@end html} is passed without change to the output "
"(i.e., suppressing the normal escaping of input @samp{<}, @samp{>} and "
"@samp{&} characters which have special significance in HTML)@.  "
"@xref{Conditional Commands}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17552
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML output, browser compatibility of"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17559
msgid ""
"@strong{Standards}: It is intentionally not our goal, and not even always "
"possible, to pass through every conceivable validation test without any "
"diagnostics.  Different validation tests have different goals, often about "
"pedantic enforcement of some standard or another.  Our overriding goal is to "
"help users, not blindly comply with standards."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17563
msgid ""
"Please report output from an error-free run of @command{texi2any} which has "
"@emph{practical} browser or EPUB reader portability problems as a bug "
"(@pxref{Reporting Bugs})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17570
msgid ""
"In practice, the HTML produced by @command{texi2any} is slowly adjusted over "
"time towards the latest HTML standard, while also trying to keep "
"compatibility with earlier produced HTML.  We use transitional markup and "
"try to be slow enough to give time for the diverse HTML readers to adjust "
"(and for standards to reincorporate useful features that were "
"dropped@dots{})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17572
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17574
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Splitting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17574
msgid "@nodedescription How HTML output is split."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17575
#, no-wrap
msgid "Split HTML output"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17576
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML output, split"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17581
msgid ""
"When splitting output at nodes (which is the default), @command{texi2any} "
"writes HTML output into (basically) one output file per Texinfo source "
"@code{@@node}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17589
msgid ""
"Each output file name is the node name with spaces replaced by @samp{-}'s "
"and special characters changed to @samp{_} followed by their code point in "
"hex (@pxref{HTML Xref}).  This is to make it portable and easy to use as a "
"file name.  In the unusual case of two different nodes having the same name "
"after this treatment, they are written consecutively to the same file, with "
"HTML anchors so each can be referred to independently."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17596
msgid ""
"If @command{texi2any} is run on a system which does not distinguish case in "
"file names, nodes which are the same except for case (e.g., @samp{index} and "
"@samp{Index}) will also be folded into the same output file with anchors.  "
"You can also pretend to be on a case insensitive filesystem by setting the "
"customization variable @code{CASE_INSENSITIVE_FILENAMES}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17604
msgid ""
"It is also possible to split at chapters or sections with @option{--split} "
"(@pxref{Invoking @command{texi2any}}).  In that case, the file names are "
"constructed after the name of the node associated with the relevant "
"sectioning command.  Also, unless @option{--no-node-files} is specified, a "
"redirection file is output for every node in order to more reliably support "
"cross-references to that manual (@pxref{HTML Xref})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17611
msgid ""
"When splitting, the HTML output files are written into a subdirectory.  The "
"subdirectory name is derived from the base name (that is, any extension is "
"removed), with @code{_html} postpended. For example, HTML output for "
"@file{gcc.texi} would be written into a subdirectory named "
"@samp{gcc_html/}.  The subdirectory name is based on @code{@@setfilename} or "
"on the input file name (@pxref{Setting the Output File Name})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17614
msgid "is always named @samp{index.html}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17620
msgid ""
"Monolithic output (@code{--no-split}) is named according to "
"@code{@@setfilename}, if present (with any @samp{.info} extension replaced "
"with @samp{.html}), @code{--output} (the argument is used literally), or "
"based on the input file name as a last resort (@pxref{Setting the Output "
"File Name})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17622
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17624
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML CSS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17624
msgid "@nodedescription Influencing HTML output with Cascading Style Sheets."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17625
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML, and CSS"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17626
#, no-wrap
msgid "CSS, and HTML output"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17627
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cascading Style Sheets, and HTML output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17632
msgid ""
"Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) is an Internet standard for influencing the "
"display of HTML documents: see @uref{http://www.w3.org/Style/CSS/}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17635
msgid ""
"By default, some CSS code is included to better implement the appearance of "
"some Texinfo environments.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17638
#, no-wrap
msgid "pre.display @{ font-family:inherit @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17643
msgid ""
"The above tells the web browser to use the same font as the main document "
"inside @samp{<pre>} elements used for @code{@@display} environments.  By "
"default, the HTML @samp{<pre>} command uses a monospaced font."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17645
msgid "Please see the reference above for a full explanation of CSS."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17649
msgid ""
"You can influence the CSS in the HTML output with two @command{texi2any} "
"options: @option{--css-include=@var{file}} and @option{--css-ref=@var{url}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17653
msgid ""
"The option @option{--css-ref=@var{url}} adds to each output HTML file a "
"@samp{<link>} tag referencing a CSS at the given @var{url}.  This allows "
"using external style sheets."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17658
msgid ""
"The option @option{--css-include=@var{file}} includes the contents "
"@var{file} in the HTML output, as you might expect.  However, the details "
"are somewhat tricky, as described in the following, to provide maximum "
"flexibility."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17659
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{@@charset} specification, in CSS files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17663
msgid ""
"The CSS file first line may be a @samp{@@charset} directive.  If present, "
"this directive is used to determine the encoding of the CSS file.  The line "
"is not copied into the output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17664
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{@@import} specifications, in CSS files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17670
msgid ""
"The CSS file may begin with so-called @samp{@@import} directives, which link "
"to external CSS specifications for browsers to use when interpreting the "
"document.  Again, a full description is beyond our scope here, but we'll "
"describe how they work syntactically, so we can explain how they are handled."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17671
#, no-wrap
msgid "Comments, in CSS files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17676
msgid ""
"There can be more than one @samp{@@import}, but they have to come first in "
"the file, with only whitespace and comments interspersed, no normal "
"definitions.  Comments in CSS files are delimited by @samp{/* ... */}, as in "
"C@.  An @samp{@@import} directive must be in one of these two forms:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17680
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@import url(http://example.org/foo.css);\n"
"@@import \"http://example.net/bar.css\";\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17686
msgid ""
"The crucial characters are the @samp{@@} at the beginning and the semicolon "
"terminating the directive.  When reading the CSS file, any such "
"@samp{@@}-directive is simply copied into the output, as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17688
#, no-wrap
msgid "If @var{file} contains only normal CSS declarations, it is"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17690
msgid "included after the default CSS, thus overriding it."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17691
#, no-wrap
msgid "If @var{file} begins with @samp{@@import} specifications (see"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17697
msgid ""
"below), then the @samp{import}'s are included first (they have to come "
"first, according to the standard), and then the default CSS is included.  If "
"you need to override the default CSS from an @samp{@@import}, you can do so "
"with the @samp{!@: important} CSS construct, as in:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17699
#, no-wrap
msgid "pre.example @{ font-size: inherit ! important @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17701
#, no-wrap
msgid "If @var{file} contains both @samp{@@import} and inline CSS"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17704
msgid ""
"specifications, the @samp{@@import}'s are included first, then default CSS, "
"and lastly the inline CSS from @var{file}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17705
#, no-wrap
msgid "Any @@-directive other than @samp{@@import} and @samp{@@charset}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17708
msgid ""
"is treated as a CSS declaration, meaning the default CSS is included and "
"then the rest of the file is prepended."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17715
msgid ""
"If the CSS file is malformed or erroneous, the output is unspecified.  The "
"meaning of the CSS file is not interpreted in any way; the special @samp{@@} "
"and @samp{;} characters are looked for the text is blindly copied into the "
"output.  Comments in the CSS file may or may not be included in the output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17719
msgid ""
"In addition to the possibilities offered by CSS, @command{texi2any} has many "
"user-definable customization variables with which you can influence the HTML "
"output.  @xref{Customization Variables}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17721
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@documentdescription}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17723
msgid "@nodedescription Document summary for the HTML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17723
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@documentdescription}: Summary Text"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17725
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17731
#, no-wrap
msgid "documentdescription"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17726
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document description"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17727
#, no-wrap
msgid "Description of document"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17728
#, no-wrap
msgid "Summary of document"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17729
#, no-wrap
msgid "Abstract of document"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17730
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<meta>} HTML tag, and document description"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17739
msgid ""
"When producing HTML output for a document, a @samp{<meta>} element is "
"written in the @samp{<head>} to give some idea of the content of the "
"document.  By default, this @dfn{description} is the title of the document, "
"taken from the @code{@@settitle} command (@pxref{@code{@@settitle}}).  To "
"change this, use the @code{@@documentdescription} environment, as in:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17744
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@documentdescription\n"
"descriptive text.\n"
"@@end documentdescription\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17748
msgid ""
"This will produce the following output in the @samp{<head>} of the HTML:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17751
#, no-wrap
msgid "<meta name=description content=\"descriptive text.\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17754
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17756
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17759
#, no-wrap
msgid "Generating EPUB"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17756
msgid "@nodedescription Details on the EPUB output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17758
#, no-wrap
msgid "EPUB, generating"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17760
#, no-wrap
msgid "Outputting EPUB"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17766
msgid ""
"EPUB is a format designed for reading electronic books on portable devices.  "
"@command{texi2any} generated EPUB 3.2 in 2022.  An EPUB file is a ZIP "
"archive container, holding informative files as well as the manual rendered "
"in HTML."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17767
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{Archive::Zip}, for EPUB file output"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17768
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"EPUB_CREATE_CONTAINER_FILE @subentry @r{avoiding} @code{Archive::Zip} "
"@r{dependency}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17775
msgid ""
"The generation of the EPUB file depends on the @code{Archive::Zip} Perl "
"module being installed.  This dependency is checked at runtime.  In the "
"default case, trying to output EPUB without this dependency raises an "
"error.  However, if the EPUB output file is not generated, with the "
"customization variable @code{EPUB_CREATE_CONTAINER_FILE} set to 0, it is not "
"an error if @code{Archive::Zip} is not installed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17781
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} tests related to EPUB generation do not require the "
"installation of @code{Archive::Zip}, as they set "
"@code{EPUB_CREATE_CONTAINER_FILE} to 0 and keep the directory containing the "
"files and directories needed for the EPUB file by setting the "
"@code{EPUB_KEEP_CONTAINER_FOLDER} customization variable to 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17783
#, no-wrap
msgid "EPUB Output File and Directory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17785
msgid "@nodedescription Use syntax highlighting in code excerpts."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17785
#, no-wrap
msgid "Container Directory and Output Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17787
#, no-wrap
msgid "Container directory for EPUB"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17788
#, no-wrap
msgid "EPUB Container directory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17800
msgid ""
"A directory containing the files and directories needed for the EPUB format "
"is created when outputting EPUB.  The name of this container directory is "
"derived from the base name of the input file (that is, any extension is "
"removed), with @code{_epub_package} postpended.  If an output directory is "
"specified, with @option{--output}, or with the @code{SUBDIR} customization "
"variable, the container directory is created in that directory instead of "
"the current directory.  At the beginning of a new run, the container "
"directory and all its contents are removed.  The container directory is also "
"removed after the final EPUB file has been generated in the default case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17804
msgid ""
"The HTML files produced from the Texinfo manual are output in subdirectories "
"of the container directory.  Image files referred to from the Texinfo "
"manual, if found, are copied to subdirectories of the container directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17805
#, no-wrap
msgid "EPUB output file"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17813
msgid ""
"The EPUB output file is a ZIP archive of the container directory.  The file "
"name is derived from the base name, with the @code{.epub} extension "
"postpended.  If an output file is specified, with @option{--output}, or with "
"the @code{OUTFILE} customization function, this file name is used instead.  "
"The output EPUB file is never placed in the directory specified by "
"@option{--output} or @code{SUBDIR}; only the container directory is placed "
"there, as explained just above."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17818
msgid ""
"The EPUB output file is not generated if the customization variable "
"@code{EPUB_CREATE_CONTAINER_FILE} is set to 0.  The container directory is "
"left after the final EPUB file has been generated if "
"@code{EPUB_KEEP_CONTAINER_FOLDER} is set."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17820
msgid "@xref{Invoking @command{texi2any}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17822
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17824
#, no-wrap
msgid "EPUB Cross-References"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17824
msgid "@nodedescription Cross-references in HTML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17835
msgid ""
"The EPUB format does not support references from an EPUB file to another "
"EPUB file.  Therefore any references to ``external'' Texinfo manuals should "
"resolve to an external URL@:.  @command{texi2any} produces these links with "
"HTML cross-reference configuration (@pxref{HTML Xref Configuration}).  Since "
"the links in the resulting EPUB are incorrect if no information is found for "
"the cross-references, @command{texi2any} issues a warning by default for "
"missing cross-references information.  If these warnings are unwanted, set "
"@code{CHECK_HTMLXREF} to 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17837
#, no-wrap
msgid "EPUB HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17838
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Generated for EPUB"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17847
msgid ""
"The HTML generated for EPUB is XHTML conformant, UTF-8 encoded, and "
"formatted without the usual HTML navigation headers and footers.  Most of "
"these features are enabled with customization variables, such as "
"@code{USE_XML_SYNTAX} or @code{OUTPUT_FILE_NAME_ENCODING}.  Some features of "
"printed output are used in EPUB.  In particular, the Top node does not "
"appear in the EPUB output, while a title page is generated.  This is "
"obtained by setting @code{NO_TOP_NODE_OUTPUT}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17857
msgid ""
"The @code{OUTFILE} and @code{SUBDIR} customization variables values "
"correspond initially to the EPUB directory container and/or the EPUB output "
"file (@pxref{EPUB Output File and Directory}).  These customization "
"variables values are undefined or reset to the locations in the container "
"directory where the XHTML files are output for the HTML generation.  It is "
"mentioned here because resetting customization variables is unusual; "
"however, the variables reset are used internally for the conversion, and "
"should not interact with any customization set by the user."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17859
msgid "@xref{HTML Customization Variables}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17861
#, no-wrap
msgid "Syntax Highlighting"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17862
#, no-wrap
msgid "Code Examples Syntax Highlighting in HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: command{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17863
#, no-wrap
msgid "source-highlight"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17868
msgid "Source highlighting is experimental, feedback is welcomed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17878
msgid ""
"Support for source code syntax highlighting is available in "
"@command{texi2any} for the HTML output, with the help of external software.  "
"This feature is turned on by setting @code{HIGHLIGHT_SYNTAX}.  Source code "
"highlighting is set up for @code{@@example} blocks.  The language specified "
"for syntax highlighting is the first argument on the @code{@@example} line "
"(@pxref{@code{@@example}}), or @code{HIGHLIGHT_SYNTAX_DEFAULT_LANGUAGE} if "
"set and there is no first argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17880
msgid ""
"The @code{HIGHLIGHT_SYNTAX} value determines the command used for "
"highlighting:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17881
#, no-wrap
msgid "highlight"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17884
msgid ""
"Use @command{highlight} from "
"@url{http://www.andre-simon.de/doku/highlight/en/highlight.php};"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17884
#, no-wrap
msgid "pygments"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17886
msgid "Use @command{pygmentize} from @url{https://pygments.org/};"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17886
#, no-wrap
msgid "anything else"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17888
msgid ""
"Use @command{source-highlight} (@pxref{,,,source-highlight, GNU "
"Source-highlight})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17891
msgid "@xref{Other Customization Variables}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17893
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Xref"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17894
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17895
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17896
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-references, in HTML output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17902
msgid ""
"Cross-references between Texinfo manuals in HTML format become standard HTML "
"@code{<a>} links.  This section describes the algorithm used, so that "
"Texinfo can cooperate with other programs, such as @command{texi2html}, by "
"writing mutually compatible HTML files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17906
msgid ""
"This algorithm may or may not be used for links @emph{within} HTML output "
"for a Texinfo file.  Since no issues of compatibility arise in such cases, "
"we do not need to specify this."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17911
msgid ""
"We try to support references to such ``external'' manuals in both monolithic "
"and split forms.  A @dfn{monolithic} (mono) manual is entirely contained in "
"one file, and a @dfn{split} manual has a file for each node.  (@xref{HTML "
"Splitting}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17914
msgid "The algorithm was primarily devised by Patrice Dumas in 2003--04."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17922
msgid "Link Basics: HTML Xref Link Basics."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17922
msgid "Node Expansion: HTML Xref Node Name Expansion."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17922
msgid "Command Expansion: HTML Xref Command Expansion."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17922
msgid "8-bit Expansion: HTML Xref 8-bit Character Expansion."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17922
msgid "Mismatch: HTML Xref Mismatch."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17922
msgid "Configuration: HTML Xref Configuration"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17925
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Xref Link Basics"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17926
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Cross-reference Link Basics"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17927
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML cross-references @subentry link basics"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17932
msgid ""
"For our purposes, an HTML link consists of four components: a host name, a "
"directory part, a file part, and a target part.  We always assume the "
"@code{http} protocol.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17935
#, no-wrap
msgid "http://@var{host}/@var{dir}/@var{file}.html#@var{target}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17941
msgid ""
"The information to construct a link comes from the node name and manual name "
"in the cross-reference command in the Texinfo source (@pxref{Cross "
"References}), and from @dfn{external information} (@pxref{HTML Xref "
"Configuration})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17943
msgid "We now consider each part in turn."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17947
msgid ""
"The @var{host} is hardwired to be the local host.  This could either be the "
"literal string @samp{localhost}, or, according to the rules for HTML links, "
"the @samp{http://localhost/} could be omitted entirely."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17955
msgid ""
"The @var{dir} and @var{file} parts are more complicated, and depend on the "
"relative split/mono nature of both the manual being processed and the manual "
"that the cross-reference refers to.  The underlying idea is that there is "
"one directory for Texinfo manuals in HTML, and a given @var{manual} is "
"either available as a monolithic file @file{@var{manual}.html}, or a split "
"subdirectory @file{@var{manual}_html/*.html}.  Here are the cases:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17961
msgid ""
"If the present manual is split, and the referent manual is also split, the "
"directory is @samp{../@var{referent}_html/} and the file is the expanded "
"node name (described later)."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17965
msgid ""
"If the present manual is split, and the referent manual is mono, the "
"directory is @samp{../} and the file is @file{@var{referent}.html}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17970
msgid ""
"If the present manual is mono, and the referent manual is split, the "
"directory is @file{@var{referent}_html/} and the file is the expanded node "
"name."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17975
msgid ""
"If the present manual is mono, and the referent manual is also mono, the "
"directory is @file{./} (or just the empty string), and the file is "
"@file{@var{referent}.html}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17985
msgid ""
"Another rule, that only holds for file names, is that base file names are "
"truncated to 245 characters, to allow for an extension to be appended and "
"still comply with the 255-character limit which is common to many "
"filesystems.  Although technically this can be changed with the "
"@code{BASEFILENAME_LENGTH} customization variable (@pxref{Other "
"Customization Variables}), doing so would make cross-manual references to "
"such nodes invalid."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17992
msgid ""
"Any directory part in the file name argument of the source cross reference "
"command is ignored.  Thus, @code{@@xref@{,,,../foo@}} and "
"@code{@@xref@{,,,foo@}} both use @samp{foo} as the manual name.  This is "
"because any such attempted hardwiring of the directory is very unlikely to "
"be useful for all the output formats that use the manual name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17994
msgid "Finally, the @var{target} part is always the expanded node name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:17998
msgid ""
"Whether the present manual is split or mono is determined by user option; "
"@command{texi2any} defaults to split, with the @option{--no-split} option "
"overriding this."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18003
msgid ""
"Whether the referent manual is split or mono, however, is another bit of the "
"external information (@pxref{HTML Xref Configuration}).  By default, "
"@command{texi2any} uses the same form of the referent manual as the present "
"manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18007
msgid ""
"Thus, there can be a mismatch between the format of the referent manual that "
"the generating software assumes, and the format it's actually present in.  "
"@xref{HTML Xref Mismatch}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18009
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Xref Node Name Expansion"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18010
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Cross-reference Node Name Expansion"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18011
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML cross-references @subentry node name expansion"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18012
#, no-wrap
msgid "node name expansion, in HTML cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18013
#, no-wrap
msgid "expansion, of node names in HTML cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18022
msgid ""
"As mentioned in the previous section, the key part of the HTML cross "
"reference algorithm is the conversion of node names in the Texinfo source "
"into strings suitable for XHTML identifiers and file names.  The "
"restrictions are similar for each: plain ASCII letters, numbers, and the "
"@samp{-} and @samp{_} characters are all that can be used.  (Although HTML "
"anchors can contain most characters, XHTML is more restrictive.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18028
msgid ""
"Cross-references in Texinfo can refer either to nodes, anchors "
"(@pxref{@code{@@anchor}}) or float labels (@pxref{@code{@@float}}).  "
"However, anchors and float labels are treated identically to nodes in this "
"context, so we'll continue to say ``node'' names for simplicity."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18032
msgid ""
"A special exception: the Top node (@pxref{The Top Node}) is always mapped to "
"the file @file{index.html}, to match web server software.  However, the HTML "
"@emph{target} is @samp{Top}.  Thus (in the split case):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18036
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@xref@{Top,,, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual@}.\n"
"@result{} <a href=\"../emacs_html/index.html#Top\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18042
msgid ""
"The standard ASCII letters (a-z and A-Z) are not modified.  All other "
"characters may be changed as specified below."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18046
msgid ""
"The standard ASCII numbers (0-9) are not modified except when a number is "
"the first character of the node name.  In that case, see below."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18050
msgid ""
"Multiple consecutive space, tab and newline characters are transformed into "
"just one space."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18053
msgid "Leading and trailing spaces are removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18057
msgid ""
"After the above has been applied, each remaining space character is "
"converted into a @samp{-} character."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18062
msgid ""
"Other ASCII 7-bit characters are transformed into @samp{_00@var{xx}}, where "
"@var{xx} is the ASCII character code in (lowercase) hexadecimal.  This "
"includes @samp{_}, which is mapped to @samp{_005f}."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18069
msgid ""
"If the node name does not begin with a letter, the literal string @samp{g_t} "
"is prefixed to the result.  (Due to the rules above, that string can never "
"occur otherwise; it is an arbitrary choice, standing for ``GNU Texinfo''.)  "
"This is necessary because XHTML requires that identifiers begin with a "
"letter."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18077
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node A  node --- with _'%\n"
"@result{} A-node-_002d_002d_002d-with-_005f_0027_0025\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18080
msgid "Example translations of common characters:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18082
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{_} @result{} @samp{_005f}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18083
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{-} @result{} @samp{_002d}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18084
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{A  node} @result{} @samp{A-node}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18094
msgid ""
"On case-folding computer systems, nodes differing only by case will be "
"mapped to the same file.  In particular, as mentioned above, Top always maps "
"to the file @file{index.html}.  Thus, on a case-folding system, Top and a "
"node named `Index' will both be written to @file{index.html}.  Fortunately, "
"the targets serve to distinguish these cases, since HTML target names are "
"always case-sensitive, independent of operating system."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18096
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Xref Command Expansion"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18097
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML Cross-reference Command Expansion"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18098
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML cross-references @subentry command expansion"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18102
msgid ""
"Node names may contain @@-commands (@pxref{Node Line Requirements}).  This "
"section describes how they are handled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18104
msgid "First, comments are removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18107
msgid ""
"Next, any @code{@@value} commands (@pxref{@code{@@set @@value}}) and macro "
"invocations (@pxref{Invoking Macros}) are fully expanded."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18110
msgid ""
"Then, for the following commands, the command name and braces are removed, "
"and the text of the argument is recursively transformed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18116
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@asis @@b @@cite @@code @@command @@dfn @@dmn @@dotless\n"
"@@emph @@env @@file @@i @@indicateurl @@kbd @@key\n"
"@@samp @@sansserif @@sc @@slanted @@strong @@sub @@sup\n"
"@@t @@U @@var @@verb @@w\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18124
msgid ""
"In addition, the following commands are replaced by constant text, as shown "
"below.  If any of these commands have non-empty arguments, as in "
"@code{@@TeX@{bad@}}, it is an error, and the result is unspecified.  In this "
"table, `(space)' means a space character and `(nothing)' means the empty "
"string.  The notation `U+@var{hhhh}' means Unicode code point @var{hhhh} (in "
"hex, as usual)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:18128
msgid ""
"There are further transformations of many of these expansions to yield the "
"final file or other target name, such as space characters to @samp{-}, etc., "
"according to the other rules."
msgstr ""

