#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20719
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-insert-@@var"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20720
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-c v"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20721
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-insert-@@var"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20724
msgid "Insert @code{@@var@{@}} and put the cursor between the braces."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20725
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-insert-@@example"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20726
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-c x"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20727
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-insert-@@example"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20730
msgid ""
"Insert @code{@@example} and put the cursor at the beginning of the next line."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20733
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-insert-braces"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20734
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-c @{"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20735
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-insert-braces"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20737
msgid "Insert @code{@{@}} and put the cursor between the braces."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20738
#, no-wrap
msgid "up-list"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20739
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c @}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20740
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c  ]"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20741
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x up-list"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20746
msgid ""
"Move from between a pair of braces forward past the closing brace.  Typing "
"@kbd{C-c ]} is easier than typing @kbd{C-c @}}, which is, however, more "
"mnemonic; hence the two keybindings.  (Also, you can move out from between "
"braces by typing @kbd{C-f}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20758
msgid ""
"To put a command such as @w{@code{@@code@{@dots{}@}}} around an "
"@emph{existing} word, position the cursor in front of the word and type "
"@kbd{C-u 1 C-c C-c c}.  This makes it easy to edit existing plain text.  The "
"value of the prefix argument tells Emacs how many words following point to "
"include between braces---@samp{1} for one word, @samp{2} for two words, and "
"so on.  Use a negative argument to enclose the previous word or words.  If "
"you do not specify a prefix argument, Emacs inserts the @@-command string "
"and positions the cursor between the braces.  This feature works only for "
"those @@-commands that operate on a word or words within one line, such as "
"@code{@@kbd} and @code{@@var}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20764
msgid ""
"This set of insert commands was created after analyzing the frequency with "
"which different @@-commands are used in the @cite{GNU Emacs Manual} and the "
"@cite{GDB Manual}.  If you wish to add your own insert commands, you can "
"bind a keyboard macro to a key, use abbreviations, or extend the code in "
"@file{texinfo.el}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20765
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-start-menu-description"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20766
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu description, start"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20767
#, no-wrap
msgid "Description for menu, start"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20775
msgid ""
"@kbd{C-c C-c C-d} (@code{texinfo-start-menu-description}) is an insert "
"command that works differently from the other insert commands.  It inserts a "
"node's section or chapter title in the space for the description in a menu "
"entry line.  (A menu entry has three parts, the entry name, the node name, "
"and the description.  Only the node name is required, but a description "
"helps explain what the node is about.  @xref{Menu Parts, , The Parts of a "
"Menu}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20782
msgid ""
"To use @code{texinfo-start-menu-description}, position point in a menu entry "
"line and type @kbd{C-c C-c C-d}.  The command looks for and copies the title "
"that goes with the node name, and inserts the title as a description; it "
"positions point at the beginning of the inserted text so you can edit it.  "
"The function does not insert the title if the menu entry line already "
"contains a description."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20787
msgid ""
"This command is only an aid to writing descriptions; it does not do the "
"whole job.  You must edit the inserted text since a title tends to use the "
"same words as a node name but a useful description uses different words."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:20789
#, no-wrap
