#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22610
msgid "@nodedescription Page headings."
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22610
msgid "Headings Introduced"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22611
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footings"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22612
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page numbering"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22613
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22614
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting headings and footings"
msgstr ""

#.  HTML output also supports something like these, but in a
#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22622
msgid ""
"Most printed manuals contain headings along the top of every page except the "
"title and copyright pages.  Some manuals also contain footings. Headings and "
"footings have no meaning in Info or the other output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22628
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides two standard heading formats, one for manuals printed on "
"one side of each sheet of paper, and the other for manuals printed on both "
"sides of the paper.  By default, nothing is specified for the footing of a "
"Texinfo file, so the footing remains blank."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22631
msgid ""
"Texinfo also has several heading and footing commands that you can use to "
"generate your own heading and footing formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22639
msgid ""
"In Texinfo, headings and footings are single lines at the tops and bottoms "
"of pages; you cannot create multiline headings or footings.  Each header or "
"footer line is divided into three parts: a left part, a middle part, and a "
"right part.  Any part, or a whole line, may be left blank.  Text for the "
"left part of a header or footer line is set flushleft; text for the middle "
"part is centered; and, text for the right part is set flushright."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22641
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@headings}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22644
msgid ""
"@nodedescriptionblock An option for turning headings on and off and double "
"or single sided printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22645
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @code{@@headings} Command"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22648
msgid "headings on off"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22648
#, no-wrap
msgid "headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22655
msgid ""
"The @code{@@headings} command is rarely used.  It specifies what kinds of "
"page headings and footings to print on each page.  Usually, this is "
"controlled by the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command.  You need the "
"@code{@@headings} command only if the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command "
"does not do what you want."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22657
msgid "You can use @code{@@headings} as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22659
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings off"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22661
msgid "Turn off printing of page headings."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22662
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings single"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22664
msgid "Turn on page headings appropriate for single-sided printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22665
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings double"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22667
msgid "Turn on page headings appropriate for double-sided printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22668
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings singleafter"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22669
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings doubleafter"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22672
msgid ""
"Turn on @code{single} or @code{double} headings, respectively, after the "
"current page is output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22673
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings on"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22676
msgid ""
"Turn on page headings: @code{single} if @samp{@@setchapternewpage on}, "
"@code{double} otherwise."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22684
msgid ""
"For example, suppose you write @code{@@setchapternewpage off} before the "
"@code{@@titlepage} command to start a new chapter on the same page as the "
"end of the last chapter.  This command also causes page headers to be "
"typeset for single-sided printing.  To cause page headers to be typeset for "
"double-sided printing, write @code{@@headings double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22687
msgid ""
"You can stop any page headings at all from being generated by writing "
"@code{@@headings off} on a line of its own, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22690
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings off\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22693
#, no-wrap
msgid "Heading Format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22695
msgid "@nodedescription Standard page heading formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22695
#, no-wrap
msgid "Standard Heading Formats"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22700
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides two standard heading formats, one for manuals printed on "
"one side of each sheet of paper, and the other for manuals printed on both "
"sides of the paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22705
msgid ""
"The standard format for single-sided printing consists of a header line in "
"which the left-hand part contains the name of the chapter, the central part "
"is blank, and the right-hand part contains the page number.  A single-sided "
"page looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22715
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  _______________________\n"
" |                       |\n"
" | chapter   page number |\n"
" |                       |\n"
" | Start of text ...     |\n"
" | ...                   |\n"
" |                       |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22726
msgid ""
"In the standard double-sided format, the left part of the left-hand "
"(even-numbered) page contains the page number, the central part is blank, "
"and the right part contains the title (specified by the @code{@@settitle} "
"command).  The left part of the right-hand (odd-numbered) page contains the "
"name of the chapter, the central part is blank, and the right part contains "
"the page number.  An even-numbered page and an odd-numbered page, side by "
"side as in an open book, look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22736
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  _______________________     _______________________\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
" | page number     title |   | chapter   page number |\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
" | Start of text ...     |   | More  text ...        |\n"
" | ...                   |   | ...                   |\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22743
msgid ""
"The chapter name is preceded by the word ``Chapter'', the chapter number and "
"a colon.  This makes it easier to keep track of where you are in the manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22746
msgid ""
"Note that on pages where a new chapter starts, some text may be omitted from "
"the heading line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22760
msgid ""
"By default, nothing is specified for the footing of a Texinfo file, so the "
"footing remains blank."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22763
#, no-wrap
msgid "Custom Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22765
msgid "@nodedescription How to create your own headings and footings."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22765
#, no-wrap
msgid "How to Make Your Own Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22770
msgid ""
"You can use the standard headings provided with Texinfo or specify your "
"own.  By default, Texinfo has no footers, so if you specify them, the "
"available page size for the main text will be slightly reduced."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22773
msgid "Texinfo provides six commands for specifying headings and footings:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22777
msgid ""
"@code{@@everyheading} and @code{@@everyfooting} generate page headers and "
"footers that are the same for both even- and odd-numbered pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22780
msgid ""
"@code{@@evenheading} and @code{@@evenfooting} commands generate headers and "
"footers for even-numbered (left-hand) pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22783
msgid ""
"@code{@@oddheading} and @code{@@oddfooting} generate headers and footers for "
"odd-numbered (right-hand) pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22787
msgid ""
"You must cancel the predefined heading commands with the @code{@@headings "
"off} command before defining your own specifications."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22792
msgid ""
"Here is how to place the chapter name at the left, the page number in the "
"center, and the date at the right of every header for both even- and "
"odd-numbered pages:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22797
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@headings off\n"
"@@everyheading @@thischapter @@| @@thispage @@| @@today@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22805
msgid ""
"You need to divide the left part from the central part and the central part "
"from the right part by inserting @samp{@@|} between parts.  Otherwise, the "
"specification command will not be able to tell where the text for one part "
"ends and the next part begins."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22810
msgid ""
"Each part can contain text or @@-commands.  The text is printed as if the "
"part were within an ordinary paragraph in the body of the page.  The "
"@@-commands replace themselves with the page number, date, chapter name, or "
"whatever."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22813
msgid "Here are the six heading and footing commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22815
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22816
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22817
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyheading @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22818
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyfooting @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22823
msgid ""
"The `every' commands specify the format for both even- and odd-numbered "
"pages.  These commands are for documents that are printed on one side of "
"each sheet of paper, or for documents in which you want symmetrical headers "
"or footers."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22824
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22825
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22826
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22827
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22828
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenheading @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22829
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddheading  @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22830
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenfooting @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22831
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddfooting  @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22835
msgid ""
"The `even' and `odd' commands specify the format for even-numbered pages and "
"odd-numbered pages.  These commands are for books and manuals that are "
"printed on both sides of each sheet of paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22842
msgid ""
"Use the @samp{@@this@dots{}} series of @@-commands to provide the names of "
"chapters and sections and the page number.  You can use the "
"@samp{@@this@dots{}} commands in the left, center, or right portions of "
"headers and footers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22845
msgid "Here are the @samp{@@this@dots{}} commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22847
#, no-wrap
msgid "thispage"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22850
msgid "Expands to the current page number."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22851
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissectionname"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22854
msgid "Expands to the name of the current section."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22855
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissectionnum"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22858
msgid "Expands to the number of the current section."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22859
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissection"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22863
msgid ""
"Expands to the number and name of the current section, in the format "
"`Section 1: Title'."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22864
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischaptername"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22867
msgid "Expands to the name of the current chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22868
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischapternum"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22872
msgid ""
"Expands to the number of the current chapter, or letter of the current "
"appendix."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22873
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischapter"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22877
msgid ""
"Expands to the number and name of the current chapter, in the format "
"`Chapter 1: Title'."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22878
#, no-wrap
msgid "thistitle"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22882
msgid ""
"Expands to the name of the document, as specified by the @code{@@settitle} "
"command."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22883
#, no-wrap
msgid "thisfile"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22891
msgid ""
"For @code{@@include} files only: expands to the name of the current "
"@code{@@include} file.  If the current Texinfo source file is not an "
"@code{@@include} file, this command has no effect.  This command does "
"@emph{not} provide the name of the current Texinfo source file unless it is "
"an @code{@@include} file.  (@xref{Include Files}, for more information about "
"@code{@@include} files.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22896
msgid ""
"You can also use the @code{@@today@{@}} command, which expands to the "
"current date, in `1 Jan 1900' format."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22896
#, no-wrap
msgid "today"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22901
msgid ""
"Other @@-commands and text are printed in a header or footer just as if they "
"were in the body of a page.  It is useful to incorporate text, particularly "
"when you are writing drafts:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22907
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@headings off\n"
"@@everyheading @@emph@{Draft!@} @@| @@thispage @@| @@thischapter\n"
"@@everyfooting @@| @@| Version: 0.27: @@today@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22912
msgid ""
"Beware of overlong titles: they may overlap another part of the header or "
"footer and blot it out."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22917
msgid ""
"If you have very short chapters and/or sections, several of them can appear "
"on a single page.  You can specify which chapters and sections you want "
"@code{@@thischapter}, @code{@@thissection} and other such macros to refer to "
"on such pages as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22919
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22920
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22921
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22922
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22928
msgid ""
"The @var{ref} argument can be either @code{top} (the @code{@@this...} "
"commands will refer to the chapter/section at the top of a page) or "
"@code{bottom} (the commands will reflect the situation at the bottom of a "
"page).  These @samp{@@every...} commands specify what to do on both even- "
"and odd-numbered pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22929
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22930
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22931
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22932
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22933
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22934
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22935
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22936
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22940
msgid ""
"These @samp{@@even...} and @samp{@@odd...} commands specify what to do on "
"only even- or odd-numbered pages, respectively.  The @var{ref} argument is "
"the same as with the @samp{@@every...} commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22946
msgid ""
"Write these commands immediately after the @code{@@...contents} commands, or "
"after the @code{@@end titlepage} command if you don't have a table of "
"contents or if it is printed at the end of your manual.  These commands have "
"no effect in @LaTeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22949
msgid ""
"By default, for @TeX{}, the @code{@@this...} commands reflect the situation "
"at the bottom of a page both in headings and in footings."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22951
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@paragraphindent}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22953
msgid "@nodedescription Specify paragraph indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22953
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@paragraphindent}: Controlling Paragraph Indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22957
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indenting paragraphs, control of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22958
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paragraph indentation control"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22965
msgid ""
"The Texinfo processors may insert whitespace at the beginning of the first "
"line of each paragraph, thereby indenting that paragraph.  You can use the "
"@code{@@paragraphindent} command to specify this indentation.  Write a "
"@code{@@paragraphindent} command at the beginning of a line followed by "
"either @samp{asis} or a number:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22968
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@paragraphindent @var{indent}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22971
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:23061
msgid "The indentation is according to the value of @var{indent}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:22976
#: texinfo/texinfo-ed.7.1-en_US.texi:23066
msgid ""
"Do not change the existing indentation (not implemented in printed output)."
